{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner answers builder lead question of 09:35 (9d833aa9) on sparse replay. The verifier folds from genesis and needs a contiguous history, so a register answers its whole history, every entry, to any key that signed an entry of it within the window and to any session of a membership that one of its claims created, with no window for the session. A register holds no secret: its entries name handles, key ids, repository names and outcomes, and co-founders on one register may read each other claims. Redaction with proofs for a shared register is a later design, not gate 1. The same rule applies to the destination: a membership session reads the destination whole history, which closes the destination read gap without raising the chain bound. Builder may land both in the live-operations intake with tests and a distinguishing control each. The two authorization gaps builder found (foreign-signer root rejected by scope-local admission; carried-input authorization accepting any quoted digest) are builder to repair in that intake; the planner dispatches no cloud work on them.","body":{"decision":"register-history-whole"}}